      "proof_review": {
        "reviewed": true,
        "context_checked": true,
        "method": "Published author proof/core and local context fidelity screening",
        "note": "Select only part(2) of Theorem8.1, identical to introduction TheoremII. Read complete free-Lie universal construction and two-generator bracket support in Sections2-4, full pseudo-product quotient/derivation construction8.2, Tanaka finite-dimensionality Lemma8.1, all degree-two/three module and centralizer support8.3/8.4, Example8.2, and the full higher-depth contradiction in8.1(2). The relevant prolongation has degree-zero derivations preserving BOTH e and f; it is not the unrestricted prolongation g(m), whose contact/Cartan exceptions are in excluded part(1). Prior Tanaka/Yamaguchi simple-gradation and first-kind structure results are explicitly published background, with exact uses retained. The author provides the full iterated Jacobi expressions and nonzero 2x2 determinant. No automorphism theorem, free one-summand classification, contact example or individual depth case is counted separately."
      },
      "extra_source_paths": [
        "raw/sigma-2012-037-072/paper-038-source/sigma12-038.tex",
        "raw/sigma-2012-037-072/paper-038-source.zip"
      ],
      "statement_scope": "Theorem8.1 part(2) only, equivalently TheoremII: over C, m,n positive, mu>=2, negative algebra m is free pseudo-product of type(m,n,mu) with g_-1=e direct-sum f, dim(e)=m,dim(f)=n and [e,e]=[f,f]=0, with the stated universal property. g is the Tanaka prolongation whose g_0 is all graded derivations preserving e and f. If g_1 is nonzero, mu=2 and g is the finite-dimensional simple graded algebra of type(A_(m+n),{alpha_m,alpha_(m+1)}), i.e. sl(m+n+1) with those adjacent marked roots. No classification of the unrestricted prolongation or automorphism group is selected.",
      "admission_hold": false,
      "editorial_warning": "Source annotation, with author bytes unchanged: in the proof on page15 eta is printed as belonging to f-star, but the displayed defining coefficient eta(Z) and both ensuing identities evaluate it on Z,X in e. Those authored formulas determine the coefficient domain used in the proof; no alternative bracket formula is supplied. Proposition8.3(3) prints a degree-three formula without a depth qualifier; the selected proof uses it only in its explicitly mu>=3 branch, while the mu=2 conclusion uses degree-two data and Example8.2.",
      "proof_complete": true,
      "contexts": [],
